# Godot ↔ Web parity (source of truth)

The **web client in `src/`** is the source of truth for Loot & Lasers design and gameplay UX.

The Godot client in `loot&lasers/` must **replicate the web experience as closely as possible**. Do **not** redesign, “modernize,” simplify, or invent a new visual language unless the user explicitly asks.

## Before changing any Godot UI

1. Open the matching web implementation under `src/` (pages, `src/components/game/`, etc.).
2. Match **layout, hierarchy, colors, typography, spacing, icons, copy, animations, menus, and flow**.
3. Prefer Godot only for **performance / animation fidelity** (tweens, particles, shaders) — not for changing the design.
4. If something cannot be reproduced exactly in Godot, **say why** and propose the **closest visual + functional equivalent** — do not silently substitute a different look.

## Mapping

| Web | Godot |
|-----|--------|
| `src/pages/*`, `src/components/game/*` | `loot&lasers/Scenes/UI/*`, `Scripts/UI/*` |
| Design tokens / Tailwind theme / game CSS | `Scripts/UI/ClientUi.gd` + existing painted styles |
| Game rules / formulas | Prefer shared server authority; mirror `src/lib/` in `loot&lasers/Scripts/` only as needed |

## Hard rules

- **No drive-by redesigns** while “fixing” or “polishing.”
- **No new color themes, fonts, or layout systems** that diverge from web.
- **Copy and section structure** (e.g. Codex) must follow the web component, not a paraphrased GDScript version.
- When auditing “does it look right?”, **diff against the live web UI**, not against AI taste.

## When exact parity is blocked

Call out limits clearly (e.g. no Lucide SVG set → closest emoji/icon atlas; HTML/CSS layout → Godot containers; Framer Motion → Tween equivalents). Ship the closest match; track gaps instead of inventing a new design.
